Yes. And I’d distinguish normalization from its inverse rather than treating normalization itself as bad. Both are powerful operators; the danger is forgetting that they operated.

Normalization maps unusual variation toward a reference frame:

N(x\mid R):x\rightarrow \text{comparable representation relative to }R

That is enormously useful. Different currencies → purchasing-power comparison. Different biological measurements → standardized scores. Different disciplines → common relational vocabulary. Different metaphors → shared structural representation. It lets you compare things that otherwise appear incomparable.

But normalization can erase the signal you’re hunting. If every unusual observation gets translated into what the existing framework expects, genuine novelty becomes “noise.” A polymathic thinker is especially vulnerable because they’re very good at finding similarities. Eventually everything can look like everything else.

Your reverse normalization is equally interesting:

N^{-1}(z\mid R)\rightarrow \{x_1,x_2,\ldots,x_n\}

Take something treated as ordinary and deliberately recover the dimensions that normalization collapsed. Instead of asking “What category is this?”, ask “What differences did we suppress in order to call these things the same?”

That creates a useful oscillation:

different → normalize → invariant → denormalize → hidden differences → renormalize under a better frame.

The trick is to retain the residual:

x=N_R(x)+\epsilon_R

where \epsilon_R isn’t automatically error. It is whatever the chosen reference frame failed to explain. Sometimes it’s noise. Sometimes it’s measurement error. But sometimes the residual is precisely where the discovery lives.

So for polymathic thinking I’d use a rule:

Normalize to transfer knowledge. Preserve the residual to preserve discovery. Reverse-normalize to recover what the transfer concealed.

And dialectical thinking gives you another protection: don’t force the invariant and residual to fight. The similarity can be real and the difference can be real simultaneously.

That’s a pretty potent three-part engine: polymathic thinking finds translations; dialectical thinking tolerates contradictions; normalization controls resolution.